<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roman the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1152–<a href="/years/1205">1205</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Roman Mstislavich · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roman Mstislavich, called the Great, was prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> from 1170 and of Halych from 1199, uniting the two into the principality of Galicia-Volhynia. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Polovtsians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and for a time dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>. He was killed in battle against the Poles at Zawichost in 1205, and his son Daniel later restored his realm.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roman-the-great.jpg" alt="Roman the Great" width="320" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Лицевой летописный свод · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9F%D0%B5%D1%87%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%8C_%D0%A0%D0%BE%D0%BC%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B0_II_%D0%92%D0%B5%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Roman was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mstislav-ii-of-kiev">Mstislav Iziaslavich</a>, Grand Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, and Agnes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and his maternal grandfather was the Polish prince Boleslaw the Wrymouth. Invited by the locals to rule Novgorod in 1168, he was opposed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrei-bogolyubsky">Andrey Bogolyubsky</a>, the ruler of Vladimir and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a>, and in 1170 he defeated Andrey's son Mstislav in battle.</p><p>After his father died that year he was expelled from Novgorod and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a>, where he inherited the city of Volodymyr. In 1188 he married Predslava, daughter of the future Kyivan prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-rostislavich">Rurik Rostislavich</a>, and in the same year he took Halych after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-osmomysl">Yaroslav Osmomysl</a>, but the Hungarian king Bela III soon expelled him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik">Rurik</a> inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> in 1194 he granted Roman several towns, then withdrew the grant under pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vsevolod-the-big-nest">Vsevolod the Big Nest</a>. Roman allied with Yaroslav II Vsevolodovich of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> and with the Polish dukes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leszek-the-white">Leszek the White</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-i-of-masovia">Konrad of Masovia</a>, and was wounded fighting in a Polish quarrel. In 1196 he raided the Jotvingians, who were attacking his borders, and divorced his wife to marry a woman named Anna, whose ancestry is unclear; some theorise that she was a daughter of the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-ii-angelos">Isaac II Angelos</a>.</p><p>In 1199 he took Halych for the second time, creating the Principality of Galicia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a>. He suppressed local boyar clans and had the support of the townspeople. In 1201–1202 and 1203–1204 he led two successful campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>, who were then allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik">Rurik</a>, and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, installing his brother-in-law Rostislav Rurikovich as prince. Byzantine chroniclers applied the title autocrate to him, though there is no evidence that he took it officially.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1205 Roman broke with Leszek and Konrad for reasons that are unknown and invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. The Galician-Volhynian Chronicle says the boyar Volodyslav Kormylchych advised it. At the Battle of Zawichost his force was attacked by Polish knights and he was killed. His young sons Daniel and Vasylko were expelled from Halych by the boyars, and the throne passed to Vladimir III Igorevich. His death led to the War of the Galician Succession (1205–1245). The Galician-Volhynian chroniclers praised him as a wise ruler, while the Polish historian Jan Długosz saw a tyrant. He founded the Romanovichi branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurikids</a>, which ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> until 1340.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Roman the Great” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1199 <b>Roman Mstislavich</b> of the Principality of Volhynia took Halych and united it with his own lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-volhynia">Kingdom of Galicia-Volhynia</a></li><li><blockquote>It became the seat of an appanage of the Volhynian princes in the later 12th century, when Vsevolod Mstislavich, a brother of <b>Roman Mstislavich</b>, ruled there.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-belz">Principality of Belz</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Roman Mstislavich</b> of the Principality of Volhynia then took Halych and joined the two lands into the Kingdom of Galicia-Volhynia, though Hungary and Poland long contested Galicia.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-galicia">Principality of Galicia</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Roman Mstislavich</b>, who succeeded in 1170, was an energetic warrior who fought the Lithuanians and Cumans.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-volhynia">Principality of Volhynia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Zawichost, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.81,21.86&amp;year=1186" aria-label="Zawichost on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Roman the Great"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4561 5756h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4561 5756h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.81,21.86&amp;year=1186">What was here in 1186? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
